          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1296   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "REGENERATION AND REPAIR 1296   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Planarian Flatworms Contain Stem Cells That Can Regenerate a Whole New Body 1297   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Vertebrates Can Regenerate Entire Limbs and Organs 1298   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Stem Cells Can Be Used Clinically to Replace Lost Hematopoietic or Skin Cells 1299   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Neural Stem Cells Can Be Manipulated in Culture and Used to Repopulate a Diseased Central Nervous System 1299   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1300   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CELL REPROGRAMMING AND PLURIPOTENT STEM CELLS 1300   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Nuclei Can Be Reprogrammed by Transplantation into Foreign Cytoplasm 1301   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Reprogramming of a Transplanted Nucleus Involves Drastic Changes in Chromatin 1301   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Embryonic Stem (ES) Cells Can Generate Any Part of the Body 1302   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Core Set of Transcription Regulators Defines and Maintains the ES-Cell State 1303   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fibroblasts Can Be Reprogrammed to Create Induced Pluripotent Stem (iPS) Cells 1303   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Reprogramming Involves a Massive Upheaval of the Gene Control System 1304   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "An Experimental Manipulation of Factors That Modify Chromatin Can Increase Reprogramming Efficiencies 1305   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "ES and iPS Cells Can Be Guided to Generate Specific Adult Cell Types and Even Organoids 1306   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cells of One Specialized Type Can Be Forced to Transdifferentiate Directly into Another 1306   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "ES and iPS Cells Are Also Useful for Drug Discovery and Analysis of Disease 1308   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1309   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Problems 1310   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "References 1312"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.077,
            0.495,
            0.341,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 23 Pathogens and Infection"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.076,
            0.514,
            0.474,
            0.944
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "INTRODUCTION TO PATHOGENS 1313  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Pathogens Can Be Viruses, Bacteria, or Eukaryotes 1314  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Pathogens Interact with Their Hosts in Different Ways 1314  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Bacteria Are Diverse and Occupy a Remarkable Variety of Ecological Niches 1315  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Bacterial Pathogens Carry Specialized Virulence Genes 1317  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Bacterial Virulence Genes Encode Toxins and Secretion Systems That Deliver Effector Proteins to Host Cells 1319  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fungal and Protozoan Parasites Have Complex Life Cycles Involving Multiple Forms 1321  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "All Aspects of Viral Propagation Depend on Host-Cell Machinery 1322  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1325  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CELL BIOLOGY OF PATHOGEN INFECTION 1325  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Pathogens Breach Epithelial Barriers to Infect the Host 1326  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Pathogens That Colonize an Epithelium Must Overcome Its Protective Mechanisms 1326  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Extracellular Pathogens Use Toxins and Contact-dependent Secretion Systems to Disturb Host Cells Without Entering Them 1328  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Intracellular Pathogens Have Mechanisms for Both Entering and Leaving Host Cells 1329  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Viruses Bind to Virus Receptors at the Host-Cell Surface 1329  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Viruses Enter Host Cells by Membrane Fusion, Pore Formation, or Membrane Disruption 1330  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Bacteria Enter Host Cells by Phagocytosis 1331  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Intracellular Eukaryotic Parasites Actively Invade Host Cells 1333  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Intracellular Pathogens Escape from the Phagosome into the Cytosol 1334  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Pathogens Alter Membrane Traffic in the Host Cell to Survive and Replicate 1335  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Bacteria and Viruses Use the Host-Cell Cytoskeleton for Intracellular Movement 1338  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Microbes Manipulate Autophagy 1340  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Viruses Can Take Over the Metabolism of the Host Cell 1340  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Pathogens Can Evolve Rapidly by Antigenic Variation 1341  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Error-prone Replication Dominates Viral Evolution 1343  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Drug-resistant Pathogens Are a Growing Problem 1344  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1346"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.493,
            0.098,
            0.89,
            0.169
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE HUMAN MICROBIOTA 1347  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Human Microbiota Is a Complex Ecological System 1347  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Microbiota Influences Our Development and Health 1348  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1349  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Problems 1350  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "References 1351"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.495,
            0.186,
            0.758,
            0.216
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 24 The Innate and Adaptive Immune Systems"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.196,
            0.891,
            0.942
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chapter 24 - The Innate and Adaptive Immune Systems 1353   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE INNATE IMMUNE SYSTEM 1354   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Epithelial Surfaces Serve as Barriers to Infection 1354   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Pattern Recognition Receptors (PRRs) Recognize Conserved Features of Pathogens 1354   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "There Are Multiple Families of PRRs 1355   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Activated PRRs Trigger an Inflammatory Response at Sites of Infection 1356   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Phagocytic Cells Seek, Engulf, and Destroy Pathogens 1358   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Complement Activation Targets Pathogens for Phagocytosis or Lysis 1358   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Virus-infected Cells Take Drastic Measures to Prevent Viral Replication 1360   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Natural Killer Cells Induce Virus-infected Cells to Kill Themselves 1361   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Dendritic Cells Provide the Link Between the Innate and Adaptive Immune Systems 1362   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1362   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "OVERVIEW OF THE ADAPTIVE IMMUNE SYSTEM 1364   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "B Cells Develop in the Bone Marrow, T Cells in the Thymus 1365   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Immunological Memory Depends on Both Clonal Expansion and Lymphocyte Differentiation 1366   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Most B and T Cells Continually Recirculate Through Peripheral Lymphoid Organs 1368   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Immunological Self-tolerance Ensures That B and T Cells Do Not Attack Normal Host Cells and Molecules 1370   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1372   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "B CELLS AND IMMUNOGLOBULINS 1372   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "B Cells Make Immunoglobulins (Igs) as Both Cell-Surface Antigen Receptors and Secreted Antibodies 1373   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mammals Make Five Classes of Igs 1373   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Ig Light and Heavy Chains of Antibodies Consist of Constant and Variable Regions 1375   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Ig Genes Are Assembled from Separate Gene Segments During B Cell Development 1377   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Antigen-driven Somatic Hypermutation Fine-Tunes Antibody Responses 1379   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "B Cells Can Switch the Class of Ig They Make 1379   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1381   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "T CELLS AND MHC PROTEINS 1382   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "T Cell Receptors (TCRs) Are Ig-like Heterodimers 1382   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Activated Dendritic Cells Activate Naive T Cells 1383   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "T Cells Recognize Foreign Peptides Bound to MHC Proteins 1384   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "MHC Proteins Are the Most Polymorphic Human Proteins Known 1388   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CD4 and CD8 Co-receptors on T Cells Bind to Invariant Parts of MHC Proteins 1389   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Developing Thymocytes Undergo Positive and Negative Selection 1389   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cytotoxic T Cells Induce Infected Target Cells to Undergo Apoptosis 1391   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Effector Helper T Cells Help Activate Other Cells of the Innate and Adaptive Immune Systems 1392   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Naive Helper T Cells Can Differentiate into Different Types of Effector T Cells 1393   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Both T and B Cells Require Multiple Extracellular Signals for Activation 1394   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Cell-Surface Proteins Belong to the Ig Superfamily 1396   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Vaccination Against Pathogens Has Been Immunology's Greatest Contribution to Human Health 1396   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 1400   "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
